WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:04.208
This slide shows the coding strategy that the final
exercises build toward

00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:09.754
The requirement is one row per patient with
admission count lab count

00:00:09.925 --> 00:00:16.815
and prescription count We must also preserve
patients with no activity The

00:00:16.842 --> 00:00:22.015
solution first aggregates each child table by
patient ID At that point

00:00:22.292 --> 00:00:27.459
each summary has at most one row for each patient
Then patients

00:00:27.472 --> 00:00:32.212
is LEFT JOINed to those summaries Because the
patient table is preserved

00:00:32.475 --> 00:00:37.264
a patient with no activity remains in the result
COALESCE turns missing

00:00:37.291 --> 00:00:42.806
counts into zero Run the query and verify two types
of patients

00:00:43.095 --> 00:00:48.334
one with several activities and one with no activity
This pattern is

00:00:48.361 --> 00:00:52.137
longer than a direct three-child join but the
reasoning is much cleaner

00:00:53.074 --> 00:00:56.613
If the target grain is one patient make the child
sources one

00:00:56.627 --> 00:00:58.048
patient before joining them
